questions.answer:
JavaScript has three ways to declare variables, differing in scope, hoisting behavior, and mutability. var is function-scoped (or global if declared outside a function) and ignores block boundaries like if/for blocks; it is hoisted and auto-initialized to undefined, so reading it before its declaration line gives undefined rather than an error, and it can be redeclared and reassigned freely in the same scope. let is block-scoped (confined to the nearest {}), can be reassigned but not redeclared in the same block, and is hoisted but left uninitialized - accessing it before its declaration throws a ReferenceError (the temporal dead zone). const is block-scoped like let, must be initialized at declaration, and its binding cannot be reassigned - but this only makes the binding immutable, not the value: obj.x = 1 still works on a const object, only obj = {} would fail. In modern code, const is preferred by default, let when reassignment is needed, and var is generally avoided because of its function-scoping and hoisting footguns.

for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {}
console.log(i); // 3 - leaked out of the loop
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) {}
console.log(j); // ReferenceError - j is block scoped

The Temporal Dead Zone is the span of code between the start of a scope and the point where a let, const, or class binding is actually declared. During that window the variable technically exists (the engine is aware of it and it 'taints' the whole scope from the top), but it is uninitialized, and any attempt to read or write it throws a ReferenceError instead of returning undefined. This differs from var, which has effectively no TDZ because it is hoisted and immediately initialized to undefined.

console.log(x); // undefined
var x = 1;

console.log(y); // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'y' before initialization
let y = 1;

The TDZ exists to catch bugs early instead of silently returning undefined, and it also explains subtler behavior: even referencing an outer variable of the same name inside a block that later declares its own let/const with that name throws, because the inner declaration shadows the entire block from its start.

A closure is what happens when a function retains access to variables from its enclosing (lexical) scope even after that outer function has finished executing. Every function in JavaScript forms a closure over the scope in which it was defined, not the scope from which it's later called. A practical use case is data privacy / encapsulation - a counter factory that hides its internal state and exposes only controlled ways to change it:

function makeCounter() {
 let count = 0;
 return { increment: () => ++count, value: () => count };
}
const counter = makeCounter();
counter.increment(); counter.increment();
counter.value(); // 2 - count itself is not accessible from outside

Closures also underpin memoization, currying, debounce/throttle implementations, the module pattern, and any callback-based API that needs to carry state, such as event handlers that remember which item they belong to.

for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) { setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100); } logs 3, 3, 3. This happens because var is function-scoped, not block-scoped, so there is only a single i shared by the whole loop and by all three scheduled callbacks. By the time the timers fire (after the loop has already finished running synchronously), i has already been incremented to 3, and every callback's closure references that same final value. Switching var to let fixes it, logging 0, 1, 2 instead:

for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
 setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100);
}

This works because let is block-scoped: the specification creates a fresh binding of i for every loop iteration, so each callback's closure captures its own separate i. Before let existed, the equivalent fix with var was to wrap the body in an IIFE that captures the current value as a parameter: (function(index){ setTimeout(() => console.log(index), 100); })(i);

Unlike lexically-scoped variables, this for a regular function is determined dynamically by the call site (how the function is invoked), not where it's defined. Roughly in priority order: called with new, this is the newly created instance; called via call/apply/bind, this is whatever was explicitly passed; called as a method (obj.method()), this is the object before the dot; called as a bare function, this is undefined in strict mode or the global object in sloppy mode. Arrow functions are the exception: they have no this binding of their own at all. Instead, they capture this lexically from the enclosing scope at the moment they're defined, and it never changes no matter how the arrow function is later invoked - which is why call/apply/bind can change an arrow function's arguments but never its this.

const obj = {
 name: 'A',
 regular() { return this.name; }, // this = obj when called as obj.regular()
 arrow: () => this?.name, // this = enclosing scope's this, not obj
};

When accessing obj.prop, the engine first checks whether prop exists as an own property directly on obj. If not, it looks at obj's internal [[Prototype]] (retrievable via Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)), checks for prop there, and if still not found, continues up to that object's own prototype, and so on - this linked sequence is the prototype chain. The chain always terminates at Object.prototype, whose own prototype is null, ending the search (returning undefined if the property was never found anywhere). This is how instances of a constructor or class share methods without each instance storing its own copy: methods live once on Constructor.prototype, and every instance's chain includes that prototype object.

function Animal(name) { this.name = name; }
Animal.prototype.speak = function () { return `${this.name} makes a sound`; };
const dog = new Animal('Rex');
dog.speak(); // found via dog -> Animal.prototype -> Object.prototype -> null

Assigning obj.prop = value does not walk the chain the same way - it creates or overwrites an own property directly on obj, shadowing any inherited property of the same name rather than mutating the prototype.

All three are non-mutating, higher-order array methods that iterate via a callback but produce different results. map() returns a new array of the same length, with each element transformed by the callback. filter() returns a new, possibly shorter array containing only elements for which the callback returned truthy. reduce() collapses the array into a single accumulated value of any type by repeatedly applying (accumulator, currentValue) => newAccumulator, starting from an optional initial value. A simplified map polyfill:

Array.prototype.myMap = function (callback, thisArg) {
 const result = [];
 for (let i = 0; i < this.length; i++) {
 if (i in this) result[i] = callback.call(thisArg, this[i], i, this);
 }
 return result;
};

Real polyfills also need to skip holes in sparse arrays (the i in this check) and throw a TypeError if callback isn't a function - details that demonstrate real understanding of the spec beyond a simple loop-and-push.

A generator function, declared with function*, can pause its execution at any yield expression and resume later exactly where it left off, producing a sequence of values over time instead of computing everything at once. Calling a generator function doesn't run its body - it returns a generator object (an iterator) whose .next() method runs up to the next yield, returning { value, done }; calling .next() again resumes right after that yield.

function* idGenerator() {
 let id = 1;
 while (true) yield id++;
}
const gen = idGenerator();
gen.next().value; // 1
gen.next().value; // 2

yield solves the problem of lazily producing values on demand - including infinite sequences that would be impossible to compute eagerly. Generators are automatically iterable (usable in for...of, spread, destructuring), which is why they're the standard way to implement a custom Symbol.iterator, and they're the low-level mechanism async generators (async function*, consumed with for await...of) build on.

Event delegation attaches a single event listener to a common ancestor element instead of separate listeners on every individual child, relying on event bubbling to catch events that originate on children as they propagate up to the ancestor. Inside the handler, event.target identifies the actual element the event started on, allowing filtering for the elements that matter.

document.querySelector('#list').addEventListener('click', (e) => {
 if (e.target.tagName === 'LI') console.log('Clicked:', e.target.textContent);
});

The main benefits: far fewer listeners to create and manage, and it automatically works for elements added to the DOM after the listener was attached, without re-binding anything - the biggest advantage for dynamic lists. The caveat is that it only works for events that actually bubble; a handful of events (focus, blur, mouseenter, mouseleave) don't bubble by default, so delegation for those needs either the capturing phase or their bubbling equivalents (focusin/focusout).

Debouncing delays running a function until a specified quiet period has passed since the last time it was invoked - every new call resets the timer, so the wrapped function only actually runs once the calls stop for that long. It's the right tool when only the final state after a burst of events matters, such as a search-as-you-type input that shouldn't fire an API call on every keystroke, only once the user pauses.

function debounce(fn, delay) {
 let timeoutId;
 return function (...args) {
 clearTimeout(timeoutId);
 timeoutId = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), delay);
 };
}

Each call clears any pending timer and schedules a fresh one, so fn only executes after delay milliseconds of silence. A common follow-up is a 'leading edge' variant that runs on the first call immediately and then ignores subsequent calls until the quiet period passes, which requires tracking whether a timer is already pending rather than just resetting it.

Throttling ensures a function runs at most once within a given time window, no matter how many times it's called during that window - unlike debounce, it guarantees regular execution during a continuous burst of events rather than waiting for the burst to stop entirely. It suits high-frequency, ongoing events where periodic updates are still wanted, like scroll, mousemove, or resize handlers.

function throttle(fn, limit) {
 let inThrottle = false;
 return function (...args) {
 if (!inThrottle) {
 fn.apply(this, args);
 inThrottle = true;
 setTimeout(() => (inThrottle = false), limit);
 }
 };
}

This is the 'leading edge' style - the first call in a window runs immediately, then further calls are ignored until the cooldown elapses. A more complete implementation also supports a 'trailing edge' call so the last attempted call during the cooldown still fires with the freshest arguments once the window ends, rather than being dropped. For animation-related throttling, requestAnimationFrame is often preferred over a fixed setTimeout interval since it aligns with the browser's actual paint cycle.

A minimal recursive deep clone walks an object/array and clones each nested value, tracking already-cloned objects to handle cycles:

function deepClone(value, seen = new WeakMap()) {
 if (value === null || typeof value !== 'object') return value;
 if (seen.has(value)) return seen.get(value);
 if (value instanceof Date) return new Date(value.getTime());
 if (value instanceof Map) {
 const copy = new Map();
 seen.set(value, copy);
 value.forEach((v, k) => copy.set(deepClone(k, seen), deepClone(v, seen)));
 return copy;
 }
 const copy = Array.isArray(value) ? [] : {};
 seen.set(value, copy);
 for (const key of Object.keys(value)) copy[key] = deepClone(value[key], seen);
 return copy;
}

The edge cases that separate a naive from a robust implementation: circular references, where an object references itself directly or indirectly, will recurse forever unless already-cloned objects are tracked (typically via a WeakMap from original to clone); special built-ins like Date, Map, and Set need explicit handling since a generic property loop won't reconstruct them correctly; and functions, Symbol keys, and undefined values are usually not preserved by simpler approaches. In practice, the built-in structuredClone() already handles most of these cases (circular refs, Date, Map, Set, typed arrays) and should be preferred over a hand-rolled version or JSON.parse(JSON.stringify()) when available.

Array.prototype.flat([depth]) flattens nested arrays up to the given depth (default 1; Infinity flattens fully) into a single array. A simple recursive implementation:

function flatten(arr, depth = 1) {
 return depth > 0
 ? arr.reduce((acc, item) =>
 acc.concat(Array.isArray(item) ? flatten(item, depth - 1) : item), [])
 : arr.slice();
}

This recurses into each array element, decrementing the remaining depth, and uses concat to merge results at each level. A common follow-up is an iterative version - pushing items onto a stack and expanding arrays back onto the stack instead of the result array - to avoid recursion and its call-stack limits on very deeply nested input. flatMap() (map() immediately followed by a depth-1 flat()) is a related built-in worth mentioning, since it's more efficient than chaining .map().flat() because it avoids creating an intermediate array.

Memoization caches the result of a function call keyed by its arguments, so subsequent calls with the same arguments return the cached result instead of recomputing - an optimization for expensive, pure functions.

function memoize(fn) {
 const cache = new Map();
 return function (...args) {
 const key = JSON.stringify(args);
 if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
 const result = fn.apply(this, args);
 cache.set(key, result);
 return result;
 };
}

The JSON.stringify(args) key works for simple, serializable arguments but breaks down for objects containing functions, undefined values, or circular references; a more robust cache uses a nested Map keyed by reference for object arguments. Memoization is a poor fit when: the function is impure (its output depends on more than its arguments, e.g. current time or external state); arguments are rarely repeated, so the cache grows without ever paying off; the function is already cheap, making the caching overhead outweigh the savings; or results are large objects that would bloat memory if cached indefinitely - in that case a cache with an eviction policy (LRU, TTL, or a WeakMap for object-keyed caches) is usually needed instead of an ever-growing plain Map.

A minimal EventEmitter maintains a registry mapping event names to arrays of listener callbacks, with methods to subscribe, unsubscribe, and fire events:

class EventEmitter {
 #listeners = new Map();
 on(event, callback) {
 if (!this.#listeners.has(event)) this.#listeners.set(event, []);
 this.#listeners.get(event).push(callback);
 return this;
 }
 off(event, callback) {
 const callbacks = this.#listeners.get(event);
 if (callbacks) this.#listeners.set(event, callbacks.filter(cb => cb !== callback));
 return this;
 }
 emit(event, ...args) {
 (this.#listeners.get(event) || []).forEach(cb => cb(...args));
 return this;
 }
}

This is essentially the Observer design pattern implemented directly: emit is the subject notifying its observers (the registered callbacks) of a change. Common follow-ups interviewers ask for: a once(event, callback) method that auto-unsubscribes after the first call, guarding emit/off when there are no listeners for that event, and deciding whether listener errors should be caught individually so one throwing callback doesn't prevent the rest from running. This same on/off/emit shape underlies both the DOM's addEventListener/removeEventListener and Node.js's built-in events.EventEmitter.

Reflect is a built-in object providing static methods that mirror the internal operations objects support (get, set, has, deleteProperty, construct, and so on) - essentially exposing, as callable functions, the same default behaviors that Proxy traps intercept. Rather than being a completely separate feature, Reflect exists specifically to pair with Proxy: inside a trap, calling the matching Reflect method lets you forward an operation to the target with its correct default semantics, instead of re-implementing that behavior yourself (e.g., manually doing target[prop] = value inside a set trap can miss edge cases that Reflect.set handles correctly, like receiver semantics for inherited setters).

const handler = {
 get(target, prop, receiver) {
 if (prop === 'secret') return 'hidden';
 return Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver); // correct default behavior
 },
};

Using Reflect inside traps also avoids subtly violating a Proxy's invariants (rules the spec enforces, like not reporting a non-configurable property as deleted), which would otherwise throw a TypeError. In short: Proxy lets you intercept operations, and Reflect gives you the standard implementation of those same operations to fall back on or forward to.

An async iterator is like a regular iterator, but its next() method returns a Promise that resolves to { value, done } instead of returning that object directly - suited for sequences whose values arrive over time (paginated API results, streamed data, database cursors), rather than values that are all already in memory. An object becomes async iterable by implementing [Symbol.asyncIterator]() (the async counterpart to Symbol.iterator), and async generator functions (async function*) automatically produce objects conforming to this protocol.

async function* asyncGenerator() {
 yield 1;
 yield 2;
}
for await (const value of asyncGenerator()) {
 console.log(value); // 1, then 2
}

for await...of works on both async and sync iterables (for sync iterables, it wraps each yielded value in an implicit await, so a sync iterable that yields Promises produces resolved values, not Promise objects). It can only be used inside an async function or a module, unlike for...of. The trade-off: even over a synchronous iterable, for await...of is slower than for...of because every step goes through Promise resolution.

The iterator protocol defines how a value produces a sequence of results one at a time: an object is an iterator if it has a next() method returning { value, done } on each call. Separately, the iterable protocol defines how an object exposes its default iterator: it must have a [Symbol.iterator]() method that returns an iterator. Anything implementing [Symbol.iterator] can be used with for...of, spread syntax, destructuring, and Array.from() - this is how arrays, strings, Maps, and Sets all support these syntaxes uniformly.

const range = {
 from: 1,
 to: 3,
 [Symbol.iterator]() {
 let current = this.from;
 const last = this.to;
 return {
 next() {
 return current <= last
 ? { value: current++, done: false }
 : { value: undefined, done: true };
 },
 };
 },
};
[...range]; // [1, 2, 3]

A generator function (function*) is usually the simplest way to implement Symbol.iterator, since calling it already returns a conforming iterator: [Symbol.iterator]: function* () { yield 1; yield 2; yield 3; }.

The Module pattern uses a function's closure to create a private scope: variables and functions declared inside are inaccessible from the outside, and the function returns only the specific pieces meant to be public, typically via an IIFE so the pattern executes immediately and yields a single object.

const counter = (function () {
 let count = 0; // private
 return {
 increment() { return ++count; },
 reset() { count = 0; },
 };
})();

This was, before block scope and ES modules, the primary way to get real encapsulation and avoid leaking implementation details or helper variables into the global namespace. The Revealing Module Pattern is a refinement: define all functions and variables privately first, then return an object that simply maps public names to references to those already-defined private members, keeping the object literal at the end as a single, readable 'table of contents' of what's public, rather than mixing implementation and exposure inline. Native ES modules now provide the same encapsulation (only exported bindings are visible to importers) more directly, so the Module pattern today mainly appears in legacy code, or inside a single file/closure where you want an object with genuinely private internal state without introducing a whole separate module.

The Factory pattern centralizes object creation behind a function (or method) that decides what to instantiate and returns the result, rather than callers using new SomeClass() directly. This decouples calling code from a specific constructor/class, letting the factory choose between several related types based on input, apply shared setup logic, or hide complex construction details behind a simple interface.

function createUser(type, name) {
 switch (type) {
 case 'admin': return { name, role: 'admin', permissions: ['all'] };
 case 'guest': return { name, role: 'guest', permissions: [] };
 default: return { name, role: 'member', permissions: ['read'] };
 }
}

A Factory is a better fit than a plain constructor when: the exact type/shape to create depends on runtime input (as above), object creation involves nontrivial logic you don't want duplicated at every call site, or you want to avoid coupling calling code directly to a specific class so the underlying implementation can change later without touching every new SomeClass() call. For simple, single-shape object creation with no branching logic, a plain constructor or class is usually simpler and sufficiently clear.

XMLHttpRequest (XHR) is the older API for making HTTP requests from JavaScript, using an event-based, callback-driven interface (onreadystatechange, onload, onerror). fetch() is the modern replacement: it's Promise-based, integrates cleanly with async/await, and is designed to work with related modern features like Service Workers.

fetch(url).then(response => response.json()).then(data => console.log(data));

One notable gotcha: fetch() only rejects on network failure - a 404 or 500 response is still a 'successful' fetch from the Promise's perspective, so response.ok or response.status must be checked explicitly, whereas some XHR wrapper libraries treat non-2xx statuses as errors automatically.

To cancel an in-flight fetch, create an AbortController, pass its .signal to the fetch options, and call controller.abort() when needed:

const controller = new AbortController();
fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal }).catch(err => {
 if (err.name === 'AbortError') console.log('Request was cancelled');
});
controller.abort(); // cancels the fetch, which rejects with an AbortError

This is commonly used to cancel a stale request when a component unmounts or a new search supersedes a previous in-flight one (e.g., debounced search-as-you-type).

Normally the browser batches DOM writes and defers layout calculation until it's actually needed (right before paint). But if JavaScript reads a geometry-dependent property - like offsetWidth, offsetHeight, getBoundingClientRect(), or clientHeight - while the layout is 'dirty' (i.e., a prior write in the same script invalidated it), the browser can't return a stale answer; it must synchronously recalculate layout right then, blocking script execution. This is called a forced synchronous layout (or forced reflow). Layout thrashing happens when a loop alternates writes and reads, forcing this expensive synchronous recalculation on every single iteration instead of once:

// Thrashing: write then read, repeated per element
elements.forEach(el => {
 el.style.width = '100px'; // write - invalidates layout
 console.log(el.offsetHeight); // read - forces synchronous recalculation
});

The fix is to separate all reads from all writes: do every measurement first while layout is still clean, then apply every style change afterward, so only one recalculation is triggered instead of N.

const heights = elements.map(el => el.offsetHeight); // batch reads
elements.forEach((el, i) => { el.style.width = heights[i] + 'px'; }); // batch writes

Chrome DevTools' Performance panel flags these as 'Forced reflow' violations, and wrapping DOM writes in requestAnimationFrame is a common way to schedule them at a point aligned with the browser's own render timing.

JavaScript normally runs on a single main thread shared with the browser's UI rendering, so any long-running, CPU-intensive synchronous code blocks user interaction and can freeze the page. A Web Worker runs a separate JavaScript file on its own background thread, genuinely in parallel with the main thread, communicating with it only through message passing (postMessage()/onmessage) - workers have no access to the DOM, window, or most main-thread-only APIs, which is the trade-off for true parallelism without shared-memory race conditions.

const worker = new Worker('worker.js');
worker.postMessage({ data: largeDataset });
worker.onmessage = (e) => console.log('Result:', e.data);

Web Workers suit CPU-bound tasks: heavy computation (image/video processing, cryptographic hashing, complex data parsing or transformation), running a physics simulation, or processing a large dataset - anything that would otherwise make the UI unresponsive if run directly on the main thread. They're not the right tool for I/O-bound waiting (network requests, timers), which async/await and Promises already handle efficiently on the main thread without blocking it. Data passed via postMessage() is generally cloned (structured clone algorithm) rather than shared by reference, which has its own overhead for very large payloads - though SharedArrayBuffer exists for true shared-memory scenarios.

A Service Worker is a special type of Web Worker that runs separately from any particular page and acts as a programmable network proxy between the browser and the network, registered against an origin and a path. Once installed and activated, it can intercept every fetch request the page (or any page under its scope) makes, and decide how to respond - serve a cached response, go to the network, or some combination of both - giving fine-grained, code-driven control over caching that HTTP cache headers alone can't provide.

self.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {
 event.respondWith(
 caches.match(event.request).then(cached => cached || fetch(event.request))
 );
});

During its install event, a Service Worker commonly pre-caches the assets a site needs (HTML, CSS, JS, images) using the Cache API, enabling an 'offline-first' experience where the app can load and function even without a network connection by serving those cached assets. Because it persists independently of any open tab, a Service Worker also enables background sync (updating a cache or retrying a failed request after connectivity returns) and push notifications, both of which require the ability to run code even when the user isn't actively on the site - capabilities central to Progressive Web Apps.

All JavaScript numbers are stored as IEEE 754 double-precision floating-point values: 64 bits split into a sign bit, an 11-bit exponent, and a 52-bit mantissa (significand). Many decimal fractions - including 0.1 and 0.2 - don't have an exact finite representation in binary, the same way 1/3 has no exact finite decimal representation; they become repeating binary fractions that must be truncated to fit in 52 mantissa bits, introducing a tiny rounding error. When 0.1 and 0.2 (each already a slightly imprecise approximation) are added, the result is approximately 0.30000000000000004, not exactly 0.3 - so the strict equality check against the literal 0.3 (itself a separate, differently-rounded approximation) fails.

This isn't a JavaScript-specific bug; it's inherent to IEEE 754 and affects nearly every mainstream language that uses it. The practical fix is to never compare floating-point results with strict equality; instead check that the difference is smaller than some acceptable tolerance:

function almostEqual(a, b, epsilon = Number.EPSILON) {
 return Math.abs(a - b) < epsilon;
}

Note that Number.EPSILON (~2.22e-16) is only a safe tolerance for numbers near 1; for very large or very small numbers a fixed tolerance can be wrong in either direction, so production code (especially financial calculations) often avoids floating-point decimals altogether - working in integer cents, or using a library like decimal.js.

A getter is a function that runs automatically when a property is read, and a setter runs automatically when a property is assigned - from the caller's perspective, they look and behave exactly like a plain data property (obj.value, not obj.getValue()), but internally can compute a derived value, validate input, or trigger side effects.

class Temperature {
 #celsius = 0;
 get fahrenheit() { return this.#celsius * 9 / 5 + 32; }
 set fahrenheit(f) { this.#celsius = (f - 32) * 5 / 9; }
}
const t = new Temperature();
t.fahrenheit = 98.6; // runs the setter
t.fahrenheit; // runs the getter, returns 98.6

On plain object literals, get/set define the property directly on the created object; in classes, get/set define the accessor on the class's prototype (so it's shared across instances, unlike Object.defineProperty() applied directly to an instance). Getters/setters are useful for: computed properties derived from other private state (as above, avoiding a stale cached value), validation on write (throwing or normalizing invalid input before it's stored), lazy computation (deferring an expensive calculation until the property is actually accessed, and optionally caching it), and providing a stable public property-style API while allowing the internal representation to change freely without breaking callers.

Object.defineProperty(obj, prop, descriptor) adds a new property to an object, or reconfigures an existing one, with precise control over its behavior via a descriptor object - behavior that plain assignment (obj.prop = value) doesn't expose. Unlike plain assignment, properties defined this way default to not writable, not enumerable, and not configurable unless explicitly set otherwise, and it uses the internal [[DefineOwnProperty]] operation rather than [[Set]], meaning it does not invoke any existing setter for that property.

Object.defineProperty(obj, 'id', {
 value: 42,
 writable: false, // can the value be changed by assignment?
 enumerable: false, // does it show up in for...in / Object.keys() / JSON.stringify()?
 configurable: false, // can it be deleted, or its descriptor changed later?
});

writable: false makes assignment to the property silently fail (or throw in strict mode) without deleting or hiding it. enumerable: false hides it from iteration and serialization while leaving it directly accessible (obj.id still works) - this is how many built-in methods (like Array.prototype.map) are non-enumerable on prototypes, so they don't clutter for...in loops. configurable: false locks the property's descriptor and prevents deletion - once set, most of its own attributes (other than writable, which can still go from true to false) can never be changed again, including by a later Object.defineProperty() call. This API underlies how Object.freeze(), getters/setters, and non-enumerable built-in methods are all implemented.

Function composition combines multiple single-purpose functions into one larger function, where the output of each step becomes the input to the next - building complex behavior from small, independently-testable pieces rather than one large function that does everything.

const pipe = (...fns) => (initial) => fns.reduce((value, fn) => fn(value), initial);
const compose = (...fns) => (initial) => fns.reduceRight((value, fn) => fn(value), initial);

const double = x => x * 2;
const addOne = x => x + 1;
pipe(double, addOne)(5); // (5*2)+1 = 11, left-to-right
compose(double, addOne)(5); // (5+1)*2 = 12, right-to-left

pipe applies functions left-to-right (matching the order they're listed, which many find more readable), while compose applies them right-to-left (the traditional mathematical convention, f(g(x))) - both use Array.prototype.reduce or reduceRight to thread a single value through the chain of functions one at a time. Composition works cleanly when each function takes and returns a single value of a consistent shape; for functions needing multiple arguments, currying is typically applied first so each step still fits the one-input-one-output contract composition depends on. Popular utility libraries (Lodash's flow/flowRight, Ramda's pipe/compose) provide production-hardened versions of exactly this pattern.

Objects and arrays in JavaScript are passed by reference (more precisely, the reference itself is passed by value, but that reference points at the same underlying object) - so if a function mutates a parameter directly, that change is visible to the caller and to anything else holding a reference to that same object, not just inside the function's own scope. This creates action-at-a-distance bugs: a caller passes in an array expecting it to be unchanged, but after the call it's silently different; two parts of a program that happen to share a reference to the same object can stomp on each other's state in ways that are hard to trace; and functions become harder to test and reason about in isolation, since their effect isn't fully captured by their return value.

// Mutates the caller's array - surprising and hard to trace
function addTax(cart) {
 cart.forEach(item => item.price *= 1.1);
 return cart;
}

// Refactored: returns a new array/objects, leaves the input untouched
function addTax(cart) {
 return cart.map(item => ({ ...item, price: item.price * 1.1 }));
}

The fix is to treat function arguments as read-only by convention: create and return new copies (via spread, map/filter, or structuredClone() for deep copies) rather than mutating in place. This is exactly the pure-function discipline functional programming and libraries like Redux enforce, and it makes a function's behavior fully predictable from its inputs and return value alone, with no hidden side effects a caller has to remember to account for.

Exponential backoff retries a failing operation with an increasingly longer delay between attempts, rather than retrying immediately or at a fixed interval - this avoids hammering an already-struggling service (which would make things worse) and gives transient failures time to resolve on their own.

async function retry(fn, { retries = 3, baseDelay = 300, maxDelay = 10000 } = {}) {
 for (let attempt = 0; ; attempt++) {
 try {
 return await fn();
 } catch (error) {
 if (attempt >= retries) throw error; // out of attempts, rethrow
 const delay = Math.min(baseDelay * 2 attempt, maxDelay);
 await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, delay));
 }
 }
}

Each failed attempt roughly doubles the wait before the next try (baseDelay 2^attempt), capped at maxDelay so it doesn't grow unbounded, and the loop rethrows the final error once retries are exhausted rather than retrying forever. Two refinements matter for production use: adding random jitter to the delay (e.g., Math.random() delay) prevents many clients that all failed at the same moment from retrying in lockstep and re-overwhelming the service simultaneously (a 'thundering herd'); and selective retrying - checking whether an error is actually worth retrying (a 503 or network timeout is; a 400 Bad Request or a bug in the caller's own code is not) - since blindly retrying a non-transient failure just wastes time and delays surfacing a real error to the caller.

A Proxy wraps a target object and lets you intercept and customize fundamental operations performed on it - property reads, writes, deletion, function calls, and more - via a handler object containing trap functions. The get(target, prop, receiver) trap fires on property reads; the set(target, prop, value, receiver) trap fires on property writes and must return true/false to indicate success. If a trap isn't defined, the operation is forwarded to the target unchanged.

const validated = new Proxy({}, {
 set(target, prop, value) {
 if (prop === 'age' && typeof value !== 'number') {
 throw new TypeError('age must be a number');
 }
 target[prop] = value;
 return true;
 },
});

Practical use cases: validation (as above), logging/auditing every property access, computed or virtual properties (e.g., returning a default for missing keys instead of undefined), reactivity systems (frameworks like Vue 3 use Proxy to detect object mutations and trigger UI updates), and revocable access - Proxy.revocable() creates a proxy that can be permanently disabled later, useful for handing out time-limited or scoped access to an object.

The Singleton pattern restricts a class or module to a single shared instance and provides a global point of access to it, typically by checking whether an instance already exists before creating a new one:

class Singleton {
 constructor() {
 if (Singleton.instance) return Singleton.instance;
 Singleton.instance = this;
 }
}
new Singleton() === new Singleton(); // true

It's commonly used for shared resources like a logger, an application-wide configuration object, or a database connection pool, where having multiple independent instances would be wasteful or inconsistent. In Node.js specifically, the module cache already makes every required module effectively a singleton by default, since repeated require() calls for the same file return the same cached export.

The trade-off: a singleton is essentially global mutable state, which makes code harder to test in isolation (tests can leak state into each other through the shared instance unless it's explicitly reset), creates hidden coupling between unrelated parts of a codebase that all depend on the same instance, and can make it hard to have more than one configuration when requirements later change (e.g., needing two independent loggers). Many teams prefer explicit dependency injection of a shared instance over an implicit global singleton for exactly these testability and coupling reasons.

The Observer pattern defines a one-to-many relationship where a subject maintains a list of observers and notifies them automatically whenever its state changes, without the subject needing to know any details about those observers beyond a common notification interface. A minimal implementation:

class Subject {
 #observers = [];
 subscribe(observer) { this.#observers.push(observer); }
 unsubscribe(observer) { this.#observers = this.#observers.filter(o => o !== observer); }
 notify(data) { this.#observers.forEach(observer => observer(data)); }
}

This pattern is pervasive in everyday JavaScript, often without being labeled as such: DOM event listeners (addEventListener registers an 'observer' callback that's notified when an event fires) are a direct instance of it, as are custom pub-sub systems (an EventEmitter's on/emit is essentially subscribe/notify), reactive state libraries (RxJS Observables, Vue's and MobX's reactivity systems notify subscribed components when observed state changes), and Redux's store.subscribe(). Recognizing the pattern helps explain why these APIs share a similar subscribe/callback shape even across unrelated libraries.

Tree shaking is a bundler optimization (used by webpack, Rollup, Vite, and others) that removes exported code which nothing in the final bundle actually imports, shrinking output size. It depends entirely on ES Modules' static import/export structure: because a bundler can determine at build time exactly which named exports are used, without executing any code, it can safely drop the unused ones.

// math.js
export function square(x) { return x * x; }
export function cube(x) { return x x x; }
// main.js
import { square } from './math'; // cube is tree-shaken away

What breaks it: CommonJS's require()/module.exports, since what a module exports isn't statically determinable - bundlers can't reliably tell what's unused (a well-known consequence: reaching for lodash-es instead of lodash specifically because the latter is CommonJS and won't tree-shake); code with side effects at the module's top level (e.g., mutating a global, injecting CSS) that a bundler can't prove is safe to drop, unless the package.json explicitly marks itself sideEffects: false or lists which files do have side effects; transpiling ESM down to CommonJS before bundling (e.g., Babel's preset-env with default settings) also defeats it, so build configs typically set modules: false to preserve ESM syntax for the bundler. Tree shaking is also generally only applied in production builds, since it interferes with fast incremental rebuilds during development.

Private class fields, declared with a # prefix (#balance), are a language-enforced privacy mechanism introduced for classes: they're only accessible from inside the class body that declares them, and any attempt to access #field from outside - even just checking if it exists - throws a SyntaxError at the syntax level (accessing obj.#field outside the class isn't just disallowed at runtime, it's not even valid syntax there) or is simply undetectable, since # names aren't ordinary string properties and can't be accessed dynamically via bracket notation or reflected via Object.keys()/JSON.stringify()/for...in.

class BankAccount {
 #balance = 0;
 deposit(amount) { this.#balance += amount; }
 getBalance() { return this.#balance; }
}
const acc = new BankAccount();
acc.#balance; // SyntaxError outside the class

The leading-underscore convention (this._balance) is purely a naming convention communicating 'please treat this as internal' - it's just a regular, fully public property that anyone can read, overwrite, or enumerate; nothing in the language actually enforces the boundary, it relies entirely on developer discipline. Private fields provide genuine encapsulation the underscore convention never could, at the cost of being a newer, less universally supported syntax and being invisible to some tooling/debugging workflows that expect ordinary property names.

A tagged template literal is a template literal preceded by a function ('tag') that receives the literal's parsed pieces as arguments instead of the literal collapsing directly into a single interpolated string. The tag function receives an array of the literal string segments as its first argument, followed by the evaluated values of each ${...} expression as the remaining arguments, and can return anything - not necessarily a string.

function highlight(strings, ...values) {
 return strings.reduce((result, str, i) =>
 result + str + (values[i] !== undefined ? `<mark>${values[i]}</mark>` : ''), '');
}
const name = 'World';
highlight`Hello, ${name}!`; // 'Hello, <mark>World</mark>!'

Practical use cases: safe HTML/SQL construction, where the tag automatically escapes each interpolated value before assembling the final string (this is exactly how libraries like styled-components implement CSS-in-JS, and how some database libraries implement injection-safe query building); internationalization, where a tag function looks up a translated template based on the string segments; and the built-in String.raw tag, which returns the literal with escape sequences left un-processed (e.g., preserving \n as two literal characters rather than a newline), useful for things like regex patterns or file paths.

A pure function has two defining properties: given the same inputs, it always returns the same output (no dependency on external mutable state like the current time, a global variable, or random values), and it produces no side effects - it doesn't mutate anything outside its own scope (arguments passed by reference, global variables, the DOM, external I/O). A function that only computes and returns a value based purely on its parameters is pure; a function that also logs to the console, makes a network request, or mutates an object it was handed is not.

// Impure - mutates the input argument
function addItem(cart, item) { cart.push(item); return cart; }
// Pure - returns a new array, leaves the input untouched
function addItem(cart, item) { return [...cart, item]; }

Purity matters for predictable state management because pure functions are trivially testable (no mocking, no setup/teardown - just assert output for given input), safe to call multiple times or in any order without unintended consequences, and easy to reason about in isolation, since their behavior can never be affected by anything happening elsewhere in the program. This is why state-management libraries like Redux require reducers to be pure functions: given the same state and action, a reducer must deterministically produce the same next state, which is what makes features like time-travel debugging, predictable re-renders, and reliable testing possible.

setTimeout(fn, 0) schedules fn as a macrotask to run as soon as possible after the specified delay - but the delay is a minimum, not a guarantee, and in practice fn only runs once the current synchronous code has fully finished and the call stack is empty, because JavaScript's single thread can't interrupt currently-running code to run something else. Even with a delay of 0ms, the callback is placed at the back of the macrotask queue and has to wait for the event loop to finish the current script, drain the entire microtask queue (including any Promise callbacks queued along the way), and only then get picked up.

console.log('1');
setTimeout(() => console.log('2'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('3'));
console.log('4');
// logs: 1, 4, 3, 2 - the setTimeout callback runs last, even with 0ms delay

Additionally, browsers commonly enforce a minimum delay (historically 4ms) for deeply nested setTimeout calls, so 0 doesn't even guarantee the shortest possible wait in every situation. A common practical use of setTimeout(fn, 0) is deliberately deferring a piece of work to run 'after' the current synchronous execution finishes - for example, letting the browser paint or process pending events before continuing with a lower-priority follow-up task.

requestAnimationFrame(callback) schedules a callback to run right before the browser's next repaint, synchronized with the display's actual refresh rate (typically 60Hz, i.e. roughly every 16.7ms) - rather than at an arbitrary, developer-chosen interval like setTimeout or setInterval use.

function animate(timestamp) {
 element.style.transform = `translateX(${timestamp / 10}px)`;
 requestAnimationFrame(animate); // schedule the next frame
}
requestAnimationFrame(animate);

This matters for smoothness: setTimeout/setInterval fire at a fixed wall-clock interval regardless of whether the browser is actually ready to paint, which can cause dropped frames, visible stutter, or wasted work updating styles the browser has no intention of painting yet. requestAnimationFrame is timed by the browser itself, so updates land exactly when a new frame is about to be drawn, producing smoother motion and avoiding unnecessary intermediate work. It also automatically pauses when the tab is inactive or hidden, saving CPU and battery - something a raw setTimeout loop would keep running uselessly in the background. The caveat: the same forced-reflow risk applies inside a rAF callback if you read layout-dependent properties (offsetHeight, getBoundingClientRect()) after writing styles in the same callback, so reads should still be batched before writes even within rAF.

Object.freeze(obj) only locks obj's own top-level properties; any object referenced by one of those properties is untouched and remains fully mutable, since freeze() doesn't recurse into nested structures. A deep freeze needs to recursively walk the object graph and freeze every nested object it finds:

function deepFreeze(obj) {
 Object.getOwnPropertyNames(obj).forEach((key) => {
 const value = obj[key];
 if (value && typeof value === 'object' && !Object.isFrozen(value)) {
 deepFreeze(value); // freeze nested objects first
 }
 });
 return Object.freeze(obj);
}

const config = { api: { retries: 3 } };
deepFreeze(config);
config.api.retries = 10; // silently fails (or throws in strict mode)

This walks every own property, recursing into any value that's itself an object (and not already frozen, to avoid infinite loops on circular references), freezing depth-first so nested objects are locked before their parent. Caveats to be aware of: this implementation doesn't distinguish arrays specially (they're handled fine since Array is an object with numeric-keyed properties, but Object.getOwnPropertyNames on an array also includes 'length'), and it won't touch non-enumerable-via-getOwnPropertyNames exotic cases like Map/Set contents, which need separate handling since freezing the Map object itself doesn't freeze its entries. For robust production use, most teams reach for a maintained deep-freeze utility rather than hand-rolling this, since edge cases (circular refs, special object types) accumulate quickly.
